<section xml:id="sec-mathematical-language-notation">

  <title>Mathematical Language and Notation</title>

  <objectives>
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <ul>
      <li>explain why mathematics uses specialized notation;</li>
      <li>distinguish variables, constants, parameters, and coefficients;</li>
      <li>identify terms, factors, expressions, equations, inequalities, and identities;</li>
      <li>interpret subscripts, superscripts, grouping symbols, and fraction notation;</li>
      <li>recognize common arithmetic and comparison symbols;</li>
      <li>read introductory set, function, and logical notation;</li>
      <li>recognize commonly used Greek letters;</li>
      <li>distinguish exact equality from approximation;</li>
      <li>translate between verbal statements and symbolic expressions;</li>
      <li>read mathematical expressions aloud accurately;</li>
      <li>recognize that the meaning of a symbol depends on mathematical context.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-mathematics-as-language">
    <title>Mathematics as a Language</title>

    <p>
      Mathematics is more than a collection of calculations. It is a language
      for describing quantities, patterns, structures, relationships, and
      logical ideas.
    </p>

    <p>
      Like an ordinary language, mathematics has vocabulary, grammar,
      conventions, and rules for constructing meaningful statements. Unlike
      ordinary language, mathematical language is designed to minimize
      ambiguity.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, the sentence
      <q>A number increased by five equals twelve</q>
      can be represented compactly as
      <md>x+5=12.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The symbolic statement is shorter, but it contains the same mathematical
      relationship.
    </p>

    <p>
      Mathematical notation allows complicated ideas to be represented
      efficiently, but notation is useful only when the reader understands
      what each symbol means.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Learning Mathematical Notation</title>

      <p>
        When unfamiliar notation is introduced in this text, three pieces of
        information are especially important:
      </p>

      <p>
        1. The <em>name</em> of the symbol.
      </p>

      <p>
        2. The <em>mathematical meaning</em> of the symbol.
      </p>

      <p>
        3. The way the notation is commonly <em>read aloud</em>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-symbols-depend-context">
    <title>Symbols Depend on Context</title>

    <p>
      A mathematical symbol does not always have one universal meaning.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, the arrow
      <m>\to</m>
      is called a <em>right arrow</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Its spoken interpretation depends on context.
    </p>

    <p>
      In logic,
      <m>P\to Q</m>
      is commonly read <q><m>P</m> implies <m>Q</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      In function notation,
      <m>f:A\to B</m>
      is commonly read <q><m>f</m> maps <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Later, in calculus,
      <m>x\to a</m>
      is commonly read <q><m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, mathematical notation must always be interpreted within its
      surrounding context.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-numbers-numerals">
    <title>Numbers and Numerals</title>

    <p>
      A <em>number</em> is a mathematical object representing quantity,
      position, magnitude, or another numerical idea.
    </p>

    <p>
      A <em>numeral</em> is a written symbol or collection of symbols
      representing a number.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>7</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is a numeral representing the number seven.
    </p>

    <p>
      Different numeral systems may represent the same number differently.
    </p>

    <p>
      The development of natural, integer, rational, real, and complex
      numbers is continued in a later section on number systems.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-basic-arithmetic-symbols">
    <title>Basic Arithmetic Symbols</title>

    <p>
      Several symbols occur throughout nearly every branch of mathematics.
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Basic Arithmetic Symbols</title>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Symbol</cell>
          <cell>Name</cell>
          <cell>Common Reading</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>+</m></cell>
          <cell>plus sign</cell>
          <cell>plus</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>-</m></cell>
          <cell>minus sign</cell>
          <cell>minus</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\pm</m></cell>
          <cell>plus-or-minus sign</cell>
          <cell>plus or minus</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\mp</m></cell>
          <cell>minus-or-plus sign</cell>
          <cell>minus or plus</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\cdot</m></cell>
          <cell>multiplication dot</cell>
          <cell>times</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\times</m></cell>
          <cell>multiplication sign</cell>
          <cell>times</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\div</m></cell>
          <cell>division sign</cell>
          <cell>divided by</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>=</m></cell>
          <cell>equals sign</cell>
          <cell>equals</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\neq</m></cell>
          <cell>not-equal sign</cell>
          <cell>is not equal to</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      For example,
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>5+3=8</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is read <q>five plus three equals eight.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly,
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>12\div4=3</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is read <q>twelve divided by four equals three.</q>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-implicit-multiplication">
    <title>Implicit Multiplication</title>

    <p>
      Multiplication does not always require a visible multiplication symbol.
    </p>

    <p>
      The expressions
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>a\cdot b,\qquad a\times b,\qquad ab</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      may all represent multiplication.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus,
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>5x</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      means
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>5\cdot x.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression <m>5x</m> is commonly read <q>five <m>x</m></q>
      rather than <q>five times <m>x</m>,</q> although both describe
      multiplication.
    </p>

    <p>
      Parentheses may also indicate multiplication:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>3(x+2).</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This means that <m>3</m> multiplies the entire quantity <m>x+2</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-comparison-symbols">
    <title>Comparison Symbols</title>

    <p>
      Numbers and expressions can be compared using inequalities.
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Common Comparison Symbols</title>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Symbol</cell>
          <cell>Name</cell>
          <cell>Reading</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>&lt;</m></cell>
          <cell>less-than sign</cell>
          <cell>is less than</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>&gt;</m></cell>
          <cell>greater-than sign</cell>
          <cell>is greater than</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\leq</m></cell>
          <cell>less-than-or-equal sign</cell>
          <cell>is less than or equal to</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\geq</m></cell>
          <cell>greater-than-or-equal sign</cell>
          <cell>is greater than or equal to</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\approx</m></cell>
          <cell>approximately-equal sign</cell>
          <cell>is approximately equal to</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      For example,
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>3&lt;7</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is read <q>three is less than seven.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The statement
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>x\geq5</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is read <q><m>x</m> is greater than or equal to five.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      It may also be expressed verbally as
      <q><m>x</m> is at least five.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly,
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>x\leq5</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      may be read <q><m>x</m> is at most five.</q>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-equality-approximation">
    <title>Exact Equality and Approximation</title>

    <p>
      The equals sign
      <m>=</m>
      indicates exact equality.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>\frac12=0.5.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The approximately-equal symbol
      <m>\approx</m>
      indicates that two quantities are close but not necessarily identical.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>\pi\approx3.14159.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Here <m>\pi</m> is the lowercase Greek letter <em>pi</em>,
      pronounced <q>pie.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The statement does not claim that <m>\pi</m> equals
      <m>3.14159</m> exactly. Instead, <m>3.14159</m> is a decimal
      approximation.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Equality versus Approximation</title>

      <p>
        Use <m>=</m> when two quantities are mathematically equal.
      </p>

      <p>
        Use <m>\approx</m> when one quantity is only an approximation
        of another.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-variables">
    <title>Variables</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-variable">
      <title>Variable</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>variable</em> is a symbol representing a quantity or
          mathematical object whose value may be unknown, unspecified,
          or allowed to change.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Letters such as
      <md>x,\qquad y,\qquad t,\qquad n</md>
      are commonly used as variables.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>x+4=10</md>
      contains the variable <m>x</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Solving the equation determines the value of <m>x</m> that makes
      the equation true.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-constants">
    <title>Constants</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-constant">
      <title>Constant</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>constant</em> is a quantity regarded as fixed within a
          particular mathematical context.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Ordinary numbers such as
      <md>2,\qquad -7,\qquad \frac34</md>
      may appear as constants.
    </p>

    <p>
      Certain important mathematical constants receive special symbols.
      Examples include
      <md>\pi,\qquad e.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The constant <m>\pi</m> appears naturally in geometry and
      trigonometry.
    </p>

    <p>
      The constant <m>e</m>, pronounced simply <q><m>e</m>,</q>
      appears naturally in exponential functions, logarithms, calculus,
      probability, and differential equations.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-parameters">
    <title>Parameters</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-parameter">
      <title>Parameter</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>parameter</em> is a quantity treated as fixed while
          studying one particular mathematical object but allowed to vary
          when considering a family of related objects.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Consider
      <md>y=mx+b.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The quantities <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> may vary along a particular
      line.
    </p>

    <p>
      The values <m>m</m> and <m>b</m> determine which line is being
      considered.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus <m>m</m> and <m>b</m> can be treated as parameters.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-coefficients">
    <title>Coefficients</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-coefficient">
      <title>Coefficient</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>coefficient</em> is a numerical or symbolic factor
          multiplying another quantity.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      In
      <md>7x,</md>
      the coefficient of <m>x</m> is <m>7</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In
      <md>-3x^2,</md>
      the coefficient of <m>x^2</m> is <m>-3</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      When no coefficient is visibly written,
      <md>x=1x,</md>
      so the coefficient is <m>1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly,
      <md>-x=-1x,</md>
      so the coefficient is <m>-1</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-terms">
    <title>Terms</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-term">
      <title>Term</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>term</em> is a part of an expression separated from
          other parts by addition or subtraction.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      For example, in
      <md>5x^2-3x+7,</md>
      the terms are
      <md>5x^2,\qquad -3x,\qquad 7.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The sign attached to a term is considered part of that term.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-factors">
    <title>Factors</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-factor">
      <title>Factor</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>factor</em> is a quantity multiplied by another quantity
          in a product.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      In
      <md>6xy,</md>
      the quantities <m>6</m>, <m>x</m>, and <m>y</m> are factors.
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly,
      <md>(x+2)(x-3)</md>
      is a product of the two factors <m>x+2</m> and <m>x-3</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-mathematical-expressions">
    <title>Mathematical Expressions</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-mathematical-expression">
      <title>Mathematical Expression</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>mathematical expression</em> is a meaningful combination of
          numbers, variables, operations, functions, and grouping symbols
          representing a mathematical quantity or object.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Examples include
      <md>
        3x+5,\qquad
        x^2-4x+7,\qquad
        \frac{x+1}{x-2},\qquad
        \sqrt{x+3}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      An expression does not necessarily make a claim.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>x+5</md>
      is an expression, but it is not by itself true or false.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-fraction-notation">
    <title>Fraction Notation</title>

    <p>
      The notation
      <md>\frac{a}{b}</md>
      contains a horizontal line called a <em>fraction bar</em> or
      <em>vinculum</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression may be read
      <q><m>a</m> over <m>b</m></q>
      or
      <q><m>a</m> divided by <m>b</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The quantity above the bar is the <em>numerator</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The quantity below the bar is the <em>denominator</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus, in
      <md>\frac{7}{12},</md>
      <m>7</m> is the numerator and <m>12</m> is the denominator.
    </p>

    <p>
      The fraction bar also acts as a grouping symbol. For example,
      <md>\frac{x+3}{x-2}</md>
      means that the entire expression <m>x+3</m> is divided by the entire
      expression <m>x-2</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-grouping-symbols">
    <title>Grouping Symbols</title>

    <p>
      Grouping symbols indicate that several quantities should be treated
      together.
    </p>

    <p>
      Common grouping symbols include
      <md>(\,),\qquad [\,],\qquad \{\,\}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      These are called <em>parentheses</em>, <em>brackets</em>, and
      <em>braces</em>, respectively.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>3(x+2)</md>
      means <m>3</m> multiplies the complete expression <m>x+2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Without the parentheses,
      <md>3x+2</md>
      represents a different expression.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-subscripts">
    <title>Subscripts</title>

    <p>
      A symbol written below and to the right of another symbol is called a
      <em>subscript</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Examples include
      <md>a_1,\qquad a_2,\qquad a_3,\qquad a_n.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression
      <md>a_n</md>
      is read <q><m>a</m> sub <m>n</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Subscripts often label members of a sequence, collection, vector,
      matrix, or family of mathematical objects.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n</md>
      may represent <m>n</m> different values.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superscripts-exponents">
    <title>Superscripts and Exponents</title>

    <p>
      A symbol written above and to the right is called a
      <em>superscript</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      When the superscript represents repeated multiplication, it is called
      an <em>exponent</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>x^3=x\cdot x\cdot x.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression <m>x^3</m> is read <q><m>x</m> cubed</q> or
      <q><m>x</m> to the third power.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly,
      <md>x^2</md>
      is commonly read <q><m>x</m> squared.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The general expression
      <md>x^n</md>
      is read <q><m>x</m> to the <m>n</m>-th power.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Not every superscript represents an exponent. For example, later,
      <md>f^{-1}</md>
      may represent an inverse function rather than a reciprocal or negative
      power.
    </p>

    <p>
      Context determines the meaning.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-radical-notation">
    <title>Radical Notation</title>

    <p>
      The symbol
      <md>\sqrt{\phantom{x}}</md>
      is called the <em>radical symbol</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>\sqrt{9}=3</md>
      is read <q>the square root of nine equals three.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      More generally,
      <md>\sqrt[n]{x}</md>
      is read <q>the <m>n</m>-th root of <m>x</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The small <m>n</m> is called the <em>index</em> of the radical.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ellipses">
    <title>Ellipses</title>

    <p>
      The notation
      <md>\ldots</md>
      is called an <em>ellipsis</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      It indicates that a recognizable pattern continues.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>1,2,3,4,\ldots</md>
      means that the sequence continues in the expected manner.
    </p>

    <p>
      Centered dots,
      <md>\cdots</md>
      are often used within mathematical operations:
      <md>1+2+3+\cdots+n.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This is read
      <q>one plus two plus three and so on through <m>n</m>.</q>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-equations">
    <title>Equations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-equation">
      <title>Equation</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          An <em>equation</em> is a mathematical statement asserting that two
          expressions have equal values.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>x+3=7</md>
      states that the expression <m>x+3</m> has the same value as <m>7</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression on the left is the <em>left-hand side</em>, often
      abbreviated LHS.
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression on the right is the <em>right-hand side</em>, often
      abbreviated RHS.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-solutions-equations">
    <title>Solutions of Equations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-solution-of-equation">
      <title>Solution of an Equation</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>solution</em> of an equation is a value that makes the equation
          true when substituted for the variable.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      For
      <md>x+3=7,</md>
      the value <m>x=4</m> is a solution because
      <md>4+3=7.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-identities">
    <title>Identities</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-identity">
      <title>Identity</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          An <em>identity</em> is an equation that is true for every allowed
          value of its variables.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>(a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2</md>
      is an algebraic identity.
    </p>

    <p>
      This differs from an equation such as
      <md>x+3=7,</md>
      which is true only for particular values of <m>x</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-inequalities">
    <title>Inequalities</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-inequality">
      <title>Inequality</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          An <em>inequality</em> is a mathematical statement comparing
          quantities using an order relation such as
          <m>&lt;</m>, <m>&gt;</m>, <m>\leq</m>, or <m>\geq</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Examples include
      <md>x&gt;4,\qquad y\leq10,\qquad -3&lt;a&lt;5.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The last expression is a <em>compound inequality</em> and is read
      <q>negative three is less than <m>a</m>, and <m>a</m> is less than
      five.</q>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-mathematical-statements">
    <title>Mathematical Statements</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-mathematical-statement">
      <title>Mathematical Statement</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>mathematical statement</em>, also called a
          <em>proposition</em>, is a declarative sentence that is either true
          or false.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>5&lt;8</md>
      is a true statement.
    </p>

    <p>
      The statement
      <md>10&lt;2</md>
      is false.
    </p>

    <p>
      An expression such as
      <md>x+3</md>
      is not a statement because it does not assert anything that can be
      assigned a truth value.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-set-notation">
    <title>Introductory Set Notation</title>

    <p>
      Sets are developed formally in a later section on elementary set theory,
      but several symbols occur frequently enough to introduce here.
    </p>

    <p>
      The symbol
      <md>\in</md>
      is called the <em>membership symbol</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression
      <md>x\in A</md>
      is read <q><m>x</m> is an element of <m>A</m></q>
      or <q><m>x</m> belongs to <m>A</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The symbol
      <md>\notin</md>
      means <q>is not an element of.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus,
      <md>x\notin A</md>
      is read <q><m>x</m> is not an element of <m>A</m>.</q>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-set-builder">
    <title>Set-Builder Notation</title>

    <p>
      A set may be described by a condition.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>A=\{x\in\mathbb{R}:x&gt;0\}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Here <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is the <em>blackboard-bold capital R</em>,
      used for the set of real numbers.
    </p>

    <p>
      The colon
      <md>:</md>
      is read <q>such that</q> in this context.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore the expression is read
      <q><m>A</m> is the set of all <m>x</m> in the real numbers such that
      <m>x</m> is greater than zero.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Some authors use a vertical bar instead:
      <md>A=\{x\in\mathbb{R}\mid x&gt;0\}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Here the symbol <m>\mid</m> is also read <q>such that</q> in this
      context.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-function-notation">
    <title>Introductory Function Notation</title>

    <p>
      Functions are developed formally in a later section.
    </p>

    <p>
      If <m>f</m> is a function, then
      <md>f(x)</md>
      is read <q><m>f</m> of <m>x</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The notation does not mean <m>f</m> multiplied by <m>x</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The notation
      <md>f:A\to B</md>
      is read <q><m>f</m> maps <m>A</m> to <m>B</m></q>
      or <q><m>f</m> is a function from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The notation
      <md>x\mapsto f(x)</md>
      uses the <em>maps-to arrow</em> <m>\mapsto</m> and is read
      <q><m>x</m> maps to <m>f(x)</m>.</q>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-logical-symbols">
    <title>Introductory Logical Symbols</title>

    <p>
      Logic is developed formally in a later section on elementary logic.
    </p>

    <p>
      Several important symbols are:
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Introductory Logical Symbols</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Symbol</cell>
          <cell>Name</cell>
          <cell>Reading</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\neg</m></cell>
          <cell>negation</cell>
          <cell>not</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\land</m></cell>
          <cell>conjunction</cell>
          <cell>and</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\lor</m></cell>
          <cell>disjunction</cell>
          <cell>or</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\to</m></cell>
          <cell>implication arrow</cell>
          <cell>implies</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\leftrightarrow</m></cell>
          <cell>biconditional</cell>
          <cell>if and only if</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\therefore</m></cell>
          <cell>therefore symbol</cell>
          <cell>therefore</cell>
        </row>

      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>P\to Q</md>
      is read <q><m>P</m> implies <m>Q</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The symbol
      <md>\therefore</md>
      consists of three dots arranged in a triangle and is called the
      <em>therefore symbol</em>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-quantifiers">
    <title>Quantifiers</title>

    <p>
      The symbol
      <md>\forall</md>
      is called the <em>universal quantifier</em> and is read
      <q>for all</q> or <q>for every.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>\forall x\in\mathbb{R},\;x^2\geq0</md>
      is read
      <q>for every real number <m>x</m>, <m>x^2</m> is greater than or
      equal to zero.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The symbol
      <md>\exists</md>
      is called the <em>existential quantifier</em> and is read
      <q>there exists.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>\exists x\in\mathbb{R}\text{ such that }x^2=4</md>
      is read
      <q>there exists a real number <m>x</m> such that <m>x^2</m> equals
      four.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      These symbols are developed more carefully in a later section on
      elementary logic.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-greek-letters">
    <title>Greek Letters</title>

    <p>
      Greek letters appear throughout mathematics.
      Some of the most common are listed below.
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Common Greek Letters</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Lowercase</cell>
          <cell>Uppercase</cell>
          <cell>Name</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\alpha</m></cell>
          <cell><m>A</m></cell>
          <cell>alpha</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\beta</m></cell>
          <cell><m>B</m></cell>
          <cell>beta</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\gamma</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\Gamma</m></cell>
          <cell>gamma</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\delta</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\Delta</m></cell>
          <cell>delta</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\epsilon</m></cell>
          <cell><m>E</m></cell>
          <cell>epsilon</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\theta</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\Theta</m></cell>
          <cell>theta</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\lambda</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\Lambda</m></cell>
          <cell>lambda</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\mu</m></cell>
          <cell><m>M</m></cell>
          <cell>mu</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\Pi</m></cell>
          <cell>pi</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\rho</m></cell>
          <cell><m>P</m></cell>
          <cell>rho</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\sigma</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\Sigma</m></cell>
          <cell>sigma</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\phi</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\Phi</m></cell>
          <cell>phi</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\omega</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\Omega</m></cell>
          <cell>omega</cell>
        </row>

      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The meaning of a Greek letter depends on context just as the meaning
      of an ordinary Latin letter does.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, <m>\theta</m> often represents an angle,
      <m>\mu</m> often represents a mean or parameter, and
      <m>\sigma</m> often represents a standard deviation.
      These conventions are common but are not universal rules.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-blackboard-bold-symbols">
    <title>Common Blackboard-Bold Symbols</title>

    <p>
      Blackboard-bold letters are frequently used to denote important
      number systems.
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Common Blackboard-Bold Number Sets</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Symbol</cell>
          <cell>Name</cell>
          <cell>Typical Meaning</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\mathbb{N}</m></cell>
          <cell>blackboard-bold N</cell>
          <cell>natural numbers</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\mathbb{Z}</m></cell>
          <cell>blackboard-bold Z</cell>
          <cell>integers</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\mathbb{Q}</m></cell>
          <cell>blackboard-bold Q</cell>
          <cell>rational numbers</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\mathbb{R}</m></cell>
          <cell>blackboard-bold R</cell>
          <cell>real numbers</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\mathbb{C}</m></cell>
          <cell>blackboard-bold C</cell>
          <cell>complex numbers</cell>
        </row>

      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      These number systems are developed more carefully in the section on
      number systems.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-reading-expressions-aloud">
    <title>Reading Expressions Aloud</title>

    <p>
      Mathematical notation should be readable both visually and verbally.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>x^2+3x-4</md>
      may be read
      <q><m>x</m> squared plus three <m>x</m> minus four.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression
      <md>\frac{x+1}{x-2}</md>
      may be read
      <q>the quantity <m>x+1</m> divided by the quantity
      <m>x-2</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression
      <md>\sqrt{x+5}</md>
      may be read
      <q>the square root of the quantity <m>x+5</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Saying <q>the quantity</q> is often useful when reading grouped
      expressions because it makes the intended structure clear.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-words-to-symbols">
    <title>Translating Words into Symbols</title>

    <p>
      Mathematical language frequently requires translating verbal
      descriptions into symbolic expressions.
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Common Translation Phrases</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Words</cell>
          <cell>Possible Symbolic Form</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>the sum of <m>x</m> and <m>5</m></cell>
          <cell><m>x+5</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>the difference of <m>x</m> and <m>5</m></cell>
          <cell><m>x-5</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>the product of <m>3</m> and <m>x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>3x</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>the quotient of <m>x</m> and <m>4</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{x}{4}</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>5</m> more than <m>x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>x+5</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>5</m> less than <m>x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>x-5</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>twice <m>x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2x</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>the square of <m>x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>x^2</m></cell>
        </row>

      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Translation requires attention to mathematical structure rather than
      simply replacing words one at a time.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-translation-order">
    <title>Order Matters in Translation</title>

    <p>
      Addition and multiplication are commutative, but subtraction and
      division are not.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <q>five less than <m>x</m></q>
      means
      <md>x-5,</md>
      not
      <md>5-x.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly,
      <q>the quotient of <m>x</m> and <m>3</m></q>
      means
      <md>\frac{x}{3},</md>
      whereas
      <q>the quotient of <m>3</m> and <m>x</m></q>
      means
      <md>\frac{3}{x}.</md>
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Translation Order</title>

      <p>
        Words such as <q>less than</q> and phrases involving quotients
        require careful attention to order.
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not assume that quantities should appear symbolically in the
        same left-to-right order in which the words appear.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-language-worked-examples">
    <title>Worked Examples</title>

    <example>
      <title>Translate a Verbal Expression</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Translate
          <q>seven more than three times <m>x</m>.</q>
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Translation Steps</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Identify <q>three times <m>x</m>.</q></cell>
              <cell><m>3x</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Interpret <q>seven more than.</q></cell>
              <cell>Add <m>7</m>.</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Write the expression.</cell>
              <cell><m>3x+7</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>3x+7</md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>


    <example>
      <title>Interpret Nested Notation</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Read
          <md>\frac{(x-2)^2}{x+4}.</md>
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Reading the Expression</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Identify the numerator.</cell>
              <cell><m>(x-2)^2</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Read the numerator.</cell>
              <cell>
                <q>the quantity <m>x-2</m>, squared</q>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Identify the denominator.</cell>
              <cell><m>x+4</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Combine the reading.</cell>
              <cell>
                <q>the square of <m>x-2</m>, divided by <m>x+4</m></q>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
    </example>


    <example>
      <title>Equation or Expression?</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Classify
          <md>4x^2-7x+3</md>
          and
          <md>4x^2-7x+3=0.</md>
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Classification</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>First object</cell>
              <cell>
                No equality or comparison is asserted; it is an expression.
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Second object</cell>
              <cell>
                It asserts equality; it is an equation.
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>4x^2-7x+3</m> is an expression, while
          <m>4x^2-7x+3=0</m> is an equation.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ambiguity-parentheses">
    <title>Ambiguity and Parentheses</title>

    <p>
      Parentheses should be used whenever they improve clarity.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>\frac{x+1}{2}</md>
      is unambiguous.
    </p>

    <p>
      Writing
      <md>x+1/2</md>
      usually means
      <md>x+\frac12,</md>
      which is different.
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly,
      <md>-(x+3)</md>
      means the opposite of the entire quantity <m>x+3</m>, whereas
      <md>-x+3</md>
      means the opposite of <m>x</m>, followed by addition of <m>3</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-common-notational-errors">
    <title>Common Notational Errors</title>

    <p>
      Several errors appear frequently when mathematical notation is first
      learned.
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Confusing <m>-x^2</m> and <m>(-x)^2</m></title>

      <p>
        Exponentiation occurs before the leading negative sign:
        <md>-x^2=-(x^2).</md>
      </p>

      <p>
        But
        <md>(-x)^2=x^2.</md>
      </p>

      <p>
        For example,
        <md>-3^2=-9,\qquad (-3)^2=9.</md>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Treating <m>f(x)</m> as Multiplication</title>

      <p>
        The notation
        <md>f(x)</md>
        usually means the value of the function <m>f</m> at <m>x</m>,
        not <m>f\cdot x</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Ignoring Grouping in Fractions</title>

      <p>
        The expression
        <md>\frac{x+2}{x-5}</md>
        has numerator <m>x+2</m> and denominator <m>x-5</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Assuming a Symbol Always Means the Same Thing</title>

      <p>
        The symbol <m>\to</m> illustrates why context matters. It can be
        read as <q>implies,</q> <q>maps to,</q> or <q>approaches,</q>
        depending on the subject.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-future-notation-preview">
    <title>Notation Used Later in the Text</title>

    <p>
      As mathematics becomes more advanced, expressions become more compact.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, calculus introduces notation such as
      <md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x).</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The operator <m>\lim</m> is called the <em>limit operator</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The expression is read
      <q>the limit of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      At that point in the text, the mathematical meaning of a limit will be
      developed carefully. For now, this example illustrates an important
      reading principle.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Read Structure, Not Just Symbols</title>

      <p>
        When encountering advanced notation, identify:
      </p>

      <p>
        1. the major operator or mathematical object;
      </p>

      <p>
        2. the variables involved;
      </p>

      <p>
        3. any conditions written above, below, or beside the symbol;
      </p>

      <p>
        4. the mathematical context in which the notation appears.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-mathematical-language-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give the name and common spoken reading of each symbol:
          <md>
            \neq,\qquad
            \leq,\qquad
            \geq,\qquad
            \approx,\qquad
            \in,\qquad
            \notin.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify the variable, coefficient, and constant in
          <md>7x-12.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          List the terms of
          <md>4x^3-2x^2+9x-5.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Translate <q>six more than twice <m>x</m></q> into symbolic
          notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Translate <q>four less than <m>y</m></q> into symbolic notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Translate <q>the quotient of <m>a+3</m> and <m>b-2</m></q>
          into symbolic notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain the difference between
          <md>-x^2 \qquad\text{and}\qquad (-x)^2.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain the difference between an expression and an equation,
          and give one example of each.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain the difference among a variable, a constant, a parameter,
          and a coefficient.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Read the following expression aloud:
          <md>\frac{(x+3)^2}{\sqrt{x-1}}.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why the arrow in
          <md>
            P\to Q,\qquad
            f:A\to B,\qquad
            x\to a
          </md>
          does not have exactly the same spoken interpretation in all three
          expressions.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Translate into words:
          <md>\forall x\in\mathbb{R},\;x^2\geq0.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-mathematical-language-connections">
    <title>Section Connections</title>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Chapter Connection</title>

      <p>
        Mathematical language and notation provide the vocabulary used
        throughout the entire text.
      </p>

      <p>
        Arithmetic notation is developed further in the section on
        arithmetic foundations; number-system notation in the section on
        number systems; set notation in the section on elementary set theory;
        function notation in the section on functions; and formal logical
        notation in the section on elementary logic.
      </p>

      <p>
        New symbols introduced later will continue to be accompanied by their
        names, meanings, and common spoken readings.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

  </subsection>

</section>
